#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124378 :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380 :topic/math-academy-id 1061 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47df7b6d-fec9-535f-8b88-a117b8bfa970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy-y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321712} {:answer/id #uuid "5561e91a-bebe-5e3f-bcd9-eb69c2455d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3-xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321822} {:answer/id #uuid "67bdc89f-375e-56ea-8ea4-a17278d434c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321957} {:answer/id #uuid "735f2dc9-74b2-5218-b349-1e5f8f79f77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322048} {:answer/id #uuid "b67836fc-421d-5b22-b35b-660c1238530c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735f2dc9-74b2-5218-b349-1e5f8f79f77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322048} :answer-field/id #uuid "f868516f-34ef-5985-b18a-ffbd80bfb8cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c542ab02-5931-58f2-9193-271289d6b23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35994" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+(3-x)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (-y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+{g}^{′}(y) & =3-x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3 \\\\ g(y) & =3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=3y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=-xy+3y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be96c265-1017-528e-b2d6-bd980d7b8fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = \\sin x - \\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122967} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b0cf71-b5d4-5ffa-992c-b6eae6169fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 2\\sin x - \\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122968} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4ff845-bbcb-5b11-bcf6-43630a40f33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 3\\sin x - 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122969} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7e77a0-486c-5a56-b533-08bc37e948f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = \\sin x + \\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122970} {:answer/id #uuid "389955b0-7237-58eb-b596-06025a5afdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 2\\sin x + 3\\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122971}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c764614f-427c-5917-aeca-aca7cb940472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122966}] :question/id #uuid "6c610779-afb5-50f5-812b-1837ea05cfe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37346" :question/problem "Find $f(x, y)$ such that $∇f(x, y) = F(x, y)$, where $F(x, y) = \\cos xi + \\sin yj$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb910bef-90e1-5421-8635-db1d4004f016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb910bef-90e1-5421-8635-db1d4004f016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f37f7d6-7efc-58de-84cc-2e728fce1077" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235001}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4340b95-6cd1-5844-9535-9e98d780f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-168602" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{3}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & =3x{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc64aee-0612-5f70-8c72-8df1a1999481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263274} {:answer/id #uuid "66ba7e97-e4b3-5011-86a1-36eb79dfb963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263889} {:answer/id #uuid "6912f021-7da7-5c61-90c4-999143bf1452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263904} {:answer/id #uuid "ca52d8a2-ba6f-54b5-bcce-c59555ea401a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264768} {:answer/id #uuid "e230c0a5-63e0-55e5-b44e-b91248e79909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ba7e97-e4b3-5011-86a1-36eb79dfb963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263889} :answer-field/id #uuid "757d31c0-99cc-5826-a461-7d448963c375" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265448}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57b858a9-b536-522e-9275-67da69f513d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132077" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06efc43d-eb38-5b98-874e-9f55c0bbbdba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263062} {:answer/id #uuid "4810273b-53be-5106-85a6-d821e49d030b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263623} {:answer/id #uuid "647221ea-f3e1-5a39-9529-f097c2e5c0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263870} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2c2b5a-de03-5ff6-a222-2914886eb6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{y}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263952} {:answer/id #uuid "e825a2d0-6272-5c4f-9792-c5bc1bfd370a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e825a2d0-6272-5c4f-9792-c5bc1bfd370a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf944f96-ad7d-5769-a11c-26d605688403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b637df-9182-5d4f-997d-44163c023403" :question/math-academy-id "q-132079" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=({e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff13513-2c62-551f-b51b-384eba917a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2y{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263146} {:answer/id #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263632} {:answer/id #uuid "63500018-57ff-5df1-bcb6-85380b2c7d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263858} {:answer/id #uuid "6c452c58-6b42-5c49-8585-0f39c63c429b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263933} {:answer/id #uuid "affded30-c6e0-5d42-b054-9af00e3bf815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263632} :answer-field/id #uuid "944f1edc-f0d0-51d2-983f-32b6ee33f7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74939473-4e44-5a23-a29d-cf0a790a9021" :question/math-academy-id "q-132080" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+2y)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32cd3a46-2070-5c04-b850-8162eeb789a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348898} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d8f137-d9d9-5d7a-bf57-3e496c94821f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350047} {:answer/id #uuid "cd48e39f-f120-599e-9a6b-494a2b1af79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350251} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d34f2d-177c-57dd-a130-fa54336633eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} {:answer/id #uuid "ed148d5a-2003-5ebe-a806-ce910794f76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=4xy+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d34f2d-177c-57dd-a130-fa54336633eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c564c77-be41-5cc9-9524-b2c24202e626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350787}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3063cdc9-a985-5db1-8c3b-1970a3def78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81734" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(2{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y) & =2{x}^{2}y+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19bc219a-35b4-506a-8788-1ce7920044b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348662} {:answer/id #uuid "697a9c9c-4964-5cc5-b1e1-e9e4ce855bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349423} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d3d365-e63f-5485-9f7c-bb976d8dbf45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-\\frac{x}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350095} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcbf686-54eb-5d98-ad26-4a8fcc350136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350408} {:answer/id #uuid "e346b178-9934-5d6b-b8a5-f42de5668c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{3}y-\\frac{x}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfcbf686-54eb-5d98-ad26-4a8fcc350136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350408} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2cd2466-47dd-5469-8369-868df3afb4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb4b31d-3aca-59fb-b7c0-b708932a190d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81737" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,i+(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ g(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=\\frac{1}{y}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d51cd1-8285-536a-8208-7ac9db192459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348483} {:answer/id #uuid "3248783c-f3e7-502c-98ea-2421859e9686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348894} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb98351-4331-5e63-877c-5e62c1993a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349291} {:answer/id #uuid "dd07eefc-581e-52c8-9b46-f43da8da7576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350377} {:answer/id #uuid "edbfac28-c489-5133-bf62-26d082cbf2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb98351-4331-5e63-877c-5e62c1993a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349291} :answer-field/id #uuid "d462b30f-3414-577e-a159-9545494e03c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "988a8975-0a4f-5e3c-8020-c74ebb3ac42c" :question/math-academy-id "q-81748" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ g(y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103b348f-34f3-5535-a8f9-7d28169b37c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348588} {:answer/id #uuid "7f84441d-8ba3-55dc-9186-bdf8e2359017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=xy{e}^{xy}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349618} {:answer/id #uuid "a56f077a-269d-5153-8c08-67abd9324a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}+2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349921} {:answer/id #uuid "b19e7881-c9e2-5039-a363-ad998058af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350016} {:answer/id #uuid "bf984817-303a-595b-bcf3-132f39ff1a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b19e7881-c9e2-5039-a363-ad998058af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350016} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fbc979-ace6-5bac-be82-3f41abff1151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb698ef5-cb64-5f1c-8b17-ff6e160a3a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-81749" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{xy}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dea30ea-6d79-5529-9cdd-2d23960e3a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406662} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfaa94c-d375-5358-a83c-08ec5ee1ffba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406664} {:answer/id #uuid "1e611253-3d99-509d-a228-1da84f8f823e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406665} {:answer/id #uuid "3f756955-ff37-5042-8335-ab5bb657b76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406671} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3780f0-041a-52d5-a729-947e413446bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dea30ea-6d79-5529-9cdd-2d23960e3a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406662} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c4c17f-d3a1-54b7-85ad-332eeea0c340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98240cfd-67f4-53e3-a23f-45405606e2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132084" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fb1154-e9d5-5abf-a426-4de833dd30db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406661} {:answer/id #uuid "30f1d918-8f71-5b48-b248-54d76cf6d20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406669} {:answer/id #uuid "3e98411d-fb1f-5835-98ed-95631d5c3272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406670} {:answer/id #uuid "92d03502-0368-5753-b847-438a0c827b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406680} {:answer/id #uuid "e73fdb1a-1b58-5eba-ad8a-569d465a93c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fb1154-e9d5-5abf-a426-4de833dd30db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406661} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a04f87b-0a35-5b54-b59b-82f029326452" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3be742e-e07b-5a79-9e12-1ef7df6cb24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81736" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea3ea19-4e92-5abd-88c9-1066a9bed13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406666} {:answer/id #uuid "507422e5-32ae-5d72-ab98-c3033c109f1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3y+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406674} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb42bf5-1fac-5e8e-8246-de98ef76481f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406677} {:answer/id #uuid "83195e93-81c4-5d1c-886b-c248874b3080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406679} {:answer/id #uuid "adcd9639-c75a-551b-9cba-97a7274611c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea3ea19-4e92-5abd-88c9-1066a9bed13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406666} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e00321-0365-5097-9e12-24d700cbbfb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6e8edea-70b0-524c-abbc-330734c9a85f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37343" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the functoin $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6c4808-c2a7-5c97-8912-c4f3de3ef57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} {:answer/id #uuid "7c064342-1507-5fe3-91dc-0ff15726350b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421419} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe7055-048b-506e-b0e8-41cb887f2884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421420} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8717c4-00ef-5595-aeca-97e8db51eb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421421} {:answer/id #uuid "c86c75a8-56c7-544d-9bc9-6d9a38de128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b6c4808-c2a7-5c97-8912-c4f3de3ef57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f524e3-bd79-5da0-98c3-54cfe15b71e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421423}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49bf02f5-63a7-5afa-b61f-cb52dc9f8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-81738" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4-2xy)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2xy+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2xy+{g}^{′}(y) & =4-2xy \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =4 \\\\ g(y) & =4y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=4y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124382 :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8219c68e-f3f4-56a9-8dee-80eaf456059c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y + 2yz + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122745} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee2dd6e-a063-5ce6-ba25-1e01e48c63c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y - 3yz + Cz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122746} {:answer/id #uuid "c8899973-d846-566d-aff8-1ee753154df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = xy^{2} + 2yz + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122747} {:answer/id #uuid "2d271ef1-cc52-51d9-8679-408b178e46be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}yz + yz^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122748} {:answer/id #uuid "5833e6fd-e2cb-5015-9115-e437b9926197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y + yz + Cxz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122749}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a39a8c-dc65-5667-9694-693d1c689df2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122744}] :question/id #uuid "b00bfeb0-ca4e-5b6e-8b0c-afb0a62e4170" :question/math-academy-id "q-36977" :question/problem "Find $f(x, y, z)$ such that $∇f(x, y, z) = F(x, y, z)$, where $F(x, y, z) = 2xyi + (x^{2} + 2z)j + 2yk$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03292879-7e19-58c5-bf30-9a82b7757e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406660} {:answer/id #uuid "10ef096b-3da6-5469-9aaf-056abb9c9b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406663} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5e2bd6-42c5-529f-9f08-cbd53403ea48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406668} {:answer/id #uuid "b1384745-a3d2-57a2-83cf-2248f12bae46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406683} {:answer/id #uuid "c18a0f77-fbcd-5ceb-b30c-ca0b3feee4c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03292879-7e19-58c5-bf30-9a82b7757e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406660} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b902c9-1d56-5de9-9587-969c0111a4bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7faa34ab-beed-5e97-be56-8c5b15402d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81739" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ec042b-d1ae-56d2-9c3b-5e465e3d4cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256801} {:answer/id #uuid "1ceac5b9-64e6-5124-a6a6-85b5d1310188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257021} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd04f96-05c9-5672-a88f-0279e73ffdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+{x}^{2}z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257581} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f96662-a242-51e6-8a22-278c74c24b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258957} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc740e7-8445-5753-9183-33d9b76eb7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+yz+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ceac5b9-64e6-5124-a6a6-85b5d1310188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257021} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c753f6-334f-50c7-9748-b981b710da38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259320}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16cbb2e0-e24c-5276-8cc2-e4e9a9ed6115" :question/math-academy-id "q-124729" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{x}^{2}\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1602f189-0d28-56ae-9dd9-e128132e6ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}+yz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256980} {:answer/id #uuid "56a824e1-89ad-5b9f-9d90-0a15c3c6afec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz-y{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257529} {:answer/id #uuid "83844a24-d36a-5bde-90cc-25704c4bd024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} {:answer/id #uuid "9676b23d-58f9-5906-86c8-bc56f352ddbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258109} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfef173-d55d-5471-9bd3-2a9896819991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}z-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83844a24-d36a-5bde-90cc-25704c4bd024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf730883-1f0b-511a-85de-b890c410b87c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af3748dd-98e9-5ccb-8f93-b1501896f383" :question/math-academy-id "q-124733" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+(3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z})\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2xy{z}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) & =3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61fd2894-92e2-5f21-a8ef-3e4ce0f75c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263843} {:answer/id #uuid "79e27da1-668f-5a0d-929e-edada25fd557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264058} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4936ba-0c0b-5085-a53f-9463a50276eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy+x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264073} {:answer/id #uuid "ab37d3c3-0675-5158-a1cc-82765ce5104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f7f29-86a2-537c-90f4-0eabb7389f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab37d3c3-0675-5158-a1cc-82765ce5104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a355c26c-a760-5643-9305-ddb34ec96b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265532}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "062ec003-5729-5fe0-9819-864cef26e212" :question/math-academy-id "q-132100" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2})\\,j-2yz\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=-y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2yz & =-2yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005532ea-a569-5e6b-9518-a0150b3fb329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz{e}^{z}+x+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263006} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7694c1-e825-574d-a180-e04f18376fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+x+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264082} {:answer/id #uuid "a49fbc3b-b203-5f1b-8f40-b0bcdbe9fbb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{z}+y{x}^{2}+x-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264402} {:answer/id #uuid "c04be1f6-81c5-54c2-ba54-5b752c684e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+x+y-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264673} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d7254b-5172-5dfd-9ea1-9404e617cc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d7254b-5172-5dfd-9ea1-9404e617cc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c4e81f-33ab-54ad-9dac-693d04696388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265624}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b7d90b3-0384-5231-a89f-b7683283ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-132103" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y{e}^{z})\\,i+(x{e}^{z}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{z}-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{z}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{z}-2z & =xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25284915-404f-5375-ac86-a55e513a4466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321435} {:answer/id #uuid "30d3adbe-dcd7-5a7c-b7f4-9d8234912198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321523} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0e8e2-a744-52a6-8ad3-e46566c11f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321736} {:answer/id #uuid "acc3c980-cbdd-5610-8d47-878be5ead5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322501} {:answer/id #uuid "b7050df8-a7aa-52e2-bfb8-36dddf3c124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0e8e2-a744-52a6-8ad3-e46566c11f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321736} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c9f1ab-635a-52c8-a0fd-14bac3805bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323548}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f96b761e-8386-56f5-990d-c8300f6fbe05" :question/math-academy-id "q-36978" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,i+2yz\\,j+{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2yz)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16fb8477-c52e-58fb-b8cf-7472dfb09f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348639} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6aa838-2967-5f6d-9ea6-bf9875d95e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+xz+xy+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348670} {:answer/id #uuid "955bbc49-7db4-56b7-a02a-3e024df98843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349787} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6dc831-45cc-555d-ac7f-a066307d2999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349861} {:answer/id #uuid "d71375c9-4e61-53ba-b6da-07758eef80e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "955bbc49-7db4-56b7-a02a-3e024df98843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349787} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7814d3-6540-596e-89a1-188d1c44755f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351031}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e18f3bc6-6740-5752-becc-b988af5a59c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81740" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(y+1)\\,i+(x+z+1)\\,j+(y+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x+z+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (z+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+x+yz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cca9c71-9e8d-570c-8946-ced4f1d50328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348697} {:answer/id #uuid "2a68e94a-4173-5ae7-858e-76a0731e97f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348821} {:answer/id #uuid "58860516-bdb9-5c40-81cc-99fbd0564951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=(x+y){e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349259} {:answer/id #uuid "5cca20bd-b007-5611-a5cf-cd625413e2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349300} {:answer/id #uuid "d106b7ad-431a-5269-9f98-9101b7fb0c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a68e94a-4173-5ae7-858e-76a0731e97f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348821} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7a6d5b1-1736-5e32-bd6e-3958724c6738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351115}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e91b9e21-1500-52f9-9378-332978dc9aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81741" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(z) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z) \\\\ h(z) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2805a157-0ff6-5513-9530-54816d9d026a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348790} {:answer/id #uuid "55830456-e7d8-5981-846c-7a4fe6003b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2xyz+y-2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349229} {:answer/id #uuid "6146406d-3af2-5ae5-90e5-08d79844f945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+yz-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349337} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b8df1f-1d2d-5411-8b30-3ebb1730a80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2xyz+yz-2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349887} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f652e8-b915-5303-b15e-d17266e3544c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2xy{z}^{2}+y-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2805a157-0ff6-5513-9530-54816d9d026a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348790} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fcc368-86fe-5787-a4dc-f3208dbfc229" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98c97ef5-5ea8-5162-8c84-a1645b2982e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81751" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2}\\,i+(x{z}^{2}+z)\\,j+(2xyz+y-2)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy{z}^{2}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{z}^{2}+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz+y-2 & =2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2 \\\\ h(z) & =-2z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c1bf68-0a65-5287-b32f-c0e4dc3478f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+xz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348638} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1acbc8-d106-5b62-8d36-a7cb3a78a863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+yz+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350128} {:answer/id #uuid "d83bed69-94c4-50e6-be9a-7dcda2208ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350341} {:answer/id #uuid "f516143d-39f1-5b86-9e6a-3fe23efc5edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xy+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350590} {:answer/id #uuid "fda21360-2a77-5e5c-bdf6-2c33643d2238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda21360-2a77-5e5c-bdf6-2c33643d2238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350678} :answer-field/id #uuid "b222692b-cb3d-5740-9bf6-1564da255bac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfdbf33d-2f2f-5cc3-b43d-2537f87d3a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81752" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=(yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,i+({e}^{xz}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{xz}+x)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz{e}^{xz}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =y{e}^{xz}+xz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={e}^{xz}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z) & =zy{e}^{xz}+x \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd49044-48d7-5753-8938-92019f630b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+2y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406667} {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a1c5-e1f1-531f-8432-aafd884d17cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+2yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2af6d4-f404-59ca-99d2-24985eb2eafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406678} {:answer/id #uuid "a809662b-6dd9-5c73-b6ef-ca6fafbec01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406681} {:answer/id #uuid "b668a6c8-35b7-56c2-aa49-6197e1a607d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f2af6d4-f404-59ca-99d2-24985eb2eafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406678} :answer-field/id #uuid "419ffafb-e705-5c7d-80bf-60c8356135f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "327918f1-3cba-54fe-8e7b-c743d7b1d3cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-81755" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2x{y}^{3}z\\,i+(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2})\\,j+({x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3}z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x{y}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int {z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z) & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58749cbe-7816-57fd-a7c2-054a48490984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406675} {:answer/id #uuid "5d64b8a8-da33-5a82-92ab-d47162348e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy-{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406676} {:answer/id #uuid "b90e506d-36bb-59dc-a9d8-181dd55c4171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+z{y}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406685} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d21bb7-6a0c-5042-8d17-4fbbdd6ece2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-xy+x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406687} {:answer/id #uuid "ea19559e-ae30-58d1-a09e-446a3ce435cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea19559e-ae30-58d1-a09e-446a3ce435cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406689} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46e9509-621a-57db-9c8d-caf61cc840c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406693}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a894d767-b55b-50b3-ac91-79c05f30a84d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124731" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y)\\,i+(2y+x+z)\\,j+(y-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+x+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2y+z)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2z & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34800481-715e-5257-9d4c-7d037cac6fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}y+yz+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497915} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf158fb-4d25-5864-9cb6-eb9d3f393756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498152} {:answer/id #uuid "cd789499-1240-527d-ba30-e55b7342f064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+yz+xz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498261} {:answer/id #uuid "cdace420-7534-506a-9578-b4e2449a1989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}z+{y}^{2}z+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498262} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ae4394-1680-5b98-a6cf-4a436850cd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf158fb-4d25-5864-9cb6-eb9d3f393756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498152} :answer-field/id #uuid "2575d212-1190-514e-a919-fd516555543d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498374}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "556e4e71-69c2-58ca-8978-843d74c13525" :question/math-academy-id "q-132102" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=4xy\\,i+(2{x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 2yz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & ={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+z+C.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166716 :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34e9e6f-2888-5f5e-8d46-214f1d392b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437366} {:answer/id #uuid "cb59301c-abc9-51ad-b0e3-c4ff391b8b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a34e9e6f-2888-5f5e-8d46-214f1d392b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a4f31-7a33-5fef-82ec-37c61f532a12" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ad8b6d-306b-5c2f-9bc1-af6874eaf7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437368} {:answer/id #uuid "5afdf89b-d566-5135-9459-4a771ed47ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ad8b6d-306b-5c2f-9bc1-af6874eaf7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437368} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4bc42d9-4ade-5ab1-a4a6-2546001b1cbb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970d8e6b-fc38-53e4-90d8-b02eee99b29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437370} {:answer/id #uuid "790f5db3-f1d6-5dcd-bbeb-89e1313e0a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790f5db3-f1d6-5dcd-bbeb-89e1313e0a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437371} :answer-field/id #uuid "fabc5da5-1944-5cda-a505-51d0b2452429" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438714}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2b9923-252b-570f-aaa0-557007eb97e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104570" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5093965-6880-52c1-99d1-4b3fe1a5c833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438610} {:answer/id #uuid "46db8ede-df07-52d4-b08f-30a129c581d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5093965-6880-52c1-99d1-4b3fe1a5c833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438610} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8bbb111-e968-59e9-b2db-a637a9f734cc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8dccafb-21ba-532f-b09e-f2674009d0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438612} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6e66f0-53f1-5ecc-87b5-35c6b5302f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d6e66f0-53f1-5ecc-87b5-35c6b5302f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438613} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e852986-873b-5b33-96a5-b8861bc18ec2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de7ca771-fa92-5536-aebc-7cee5f961670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438614} {:answer/id #uuid "86c635ce-a4a6-5c29-a72f-7b9a4ef3b7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c635ce-a4a6-5c29-a72f-7b9a4ef3b7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438615} :answer-field/id #uuid "144d2103-4cd4-5e83-9245-447662f5adf6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439336}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "321a61eb-7345-5f73-a2c6-72200c42218f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81745" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({x}^{2}+2y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5650cae6-4ebd-5fab-b924-a7307b4e29a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437372} {:answer/id #uuid "6993da07-2440-58cb-93b7-f9bd99118665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6993da07-2440-58cb-93b7-f9bd99118665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e37b67f-127d-5c2b-b4d2-843eb6fcfe09" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b38472-837a-578d-bc86-3da38e6632e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437374} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3efb19-98f5-5322-9115-86ca840350d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3efb19-98f5-5322-9115-86ca840350d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18ab833-d485-51f2-bc95-50bb5a8ff3bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5941ba21-ceee-5f9b-91b5-ac8cf172b28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437376} {:answer/id #uuid "15d3424b-50ff-5a9b-a7ba-8a86b14dd910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15d3424b-50ff-5a9b-a7ba-8a86b14dd910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437377} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df86221-3e95-5646-bb83-ccb26044056c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438717}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ff94cca-f0c0-5b4f-9e96-5ccdc57e1837" :question/math-academy-id "q-104573" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65481b7b-861e-5720-a6dd-8d674d4da852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437384} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2c88c4-d301-5e5f-ada9-7861ac00371d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65481b7b-861e-5720-a6dd-8d674d4da852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437384} :answer-field/id #uuid "20435457-d381-5fe1-8f30-f9db1f097122" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "721521bd-d66b-56ad-b5bc-1f9a532924dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437386} {:answer/id #uuid "3b297167-e02b-58b6-a123-5866263ab82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b297167-e02b-58b6-a123-5866263ab82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437387} :answer-field/id #uuid "28606333-ca6f-5543-bf82-4a014fa763e8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53745e3-b275-5699-876b-c7e875b44edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437388} {:answer/id #uuid "53a6adf6-bbaa-5c44-b75e-33daf101bb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53a6adf6-bbaa-5c44-b75e-33daf101bb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437389} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd839e2a-5adc-5c75-b668-ba299a877b00" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aac0da30-e1e4-5293-838c-ddb161ffe326" :question/math-academy-id "q-104582" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(2x-4y)\\,i+(4x-8y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G={2}^{2x+y+1}\\,i+{2}^{2x+y}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-4y$ and $Q=4x-8y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={2}^{2x+y+1}$ and $Q={2}^{2x+y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ and $Q=\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ea03ce-0bea-5a33-b768-531593cecfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437710} {:answer/id #uuid "3e12d6c1-cf51-5a79-9824-a889289525f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71ea03ce-0bea-5a33-b768-531593cecfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437710} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9705fa0-051d-5108-9f33-8e0b95bf6771" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71524ab4-c1fe-56a5-b9b0-5f1247ce034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437712} {:answer/id #uuid "9809981f-9ddb-5aa8-be88-9c61eb732ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9809981f-9ddb-5aa8-be88-9c61eb732ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437713} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bc640c-07ec-54b1-ad03-d8c8b9f0a1f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425e03bd-f65f-5c5d-a11e-3e1e0b0bfa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437714} {:answer/id #uuid "6312d848-ea3c-5408-9f3b-a4a431e27732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6312d848-ea3c-5408-9f3b-a4a431e27732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437715} :answer-field/id #uuid "482a8231-c447-5ba1-b319-c14d7a80bd76" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc1786af-49ed-5f49-98af-90852a6f6e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-123984" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=3{e}^{3x+2y}$ and $Q=2{e}^{3x+2y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6{e}^{3x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6{e}^{3x+2y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac5898e-ccd9-5404-8efc-311f58a28a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438598} {:answer/id #uuid "40d3c152-dad7-5945-a794-9fb168302c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d3c152-dad7-5945-a794-9fb168302c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438599} :answer-field/id #uuid "6578c37d-0b49-5206-b245-6ec044a9e0dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec262c2d-cd4c-5f8a-9d3f-9bbc45bf6356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438600} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3825b-3dd0-5749-8394-e80247b12499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3825b-3dd0-5749-8394-e80247b12499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438601} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb8753c-ab98-5e82-82cd-ee42afcd9f28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3d211e-4ac3-517a-add9-2d442df1b48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438602} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9eb12-1057-58bb-8116-fa1399ef2296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9eb12-1057-58bb-8116-fa1399ef2296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438603} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0eaf1f-730a-5a51-9bf3-fa77e2cb6d36" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439330}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc1a89db-98a6-54e9-a27c-1ebc3462a7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81460" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})$ and $Q=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=x{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371f0d39-776e-5b85-a508-c94b2ac2b522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438604} {:answer/id #uuid "aca9ec9d-b6b0-5219-90db-431dcf2f158f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "371f0d39-776e-5b85-a508-c94b2ac2b522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438604} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dda7980-1744-5da6-8443-f5cd3aa61fb8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff09ca98-e109-5e9b-a23c-9174cdfe3d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438606} {:answer/id #uuid "95604c57-6e13-5fb9-b03a-6c3250992cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95604c57-6e13-5fb9-b03a-6c3250992cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438607} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6a548e-e667-5d89-9d10-459ef9e2e419" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063f9ca6-220f-5f24-91d4-0cff834e06d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438608} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47704e-68c1-5d9e-9f9b-802de105632e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "063f9ca6-220f-5f24-91d4-0cff834e06d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438608} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd11437-4a71-571d-b489-a706ff584c35" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2410b4fd-4172-5cce-b672-b1259e48aa6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81743" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(2x+1)\\,i+(5{y}^{2}+2y+1)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H={y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x+1$ and $Q=5{y}^{2}+2y+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3d344f4-8ed3-593a-aed4-5228049f941e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438616} {:answer/id #uuid "b51feba4-149e-552b-ba57-a034975c6254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b51feba4-149e-552b-ba57-a034975c6254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438617} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0efaccc-6c1e-515a-a6b3-27b5fea347ab" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fceaa12c-f1cb-5a20-a916-433cb5161bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438618} {:answer/id #uuid "fdca841c-e44f-5760-937f-556b1d7dbb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fceaa12c-f1cb-5a20-a916-433cb5161bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438618} :answer-field/id #uuid "75fbead4-be61-5190-81c8-ff7df1836c09" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ce1c69-5a9a-5668-a6d1-ef1043e559b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438620} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc59867-4b98-5161-bf03-738307481109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ce1c69-5a9a-5668-a6d1-ef1043e559b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438620} :answer-field/id #uuid "521321bd-40ca-5972-9cfb-572b598d4cdd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7be69df3-7414-5358-b771-962bb47016d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81746" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=xy\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(4x+{y}^{2})\\,i-4x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e78342e-7073-5996-b8d2-29a251917883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437704} {:answer/id #uuid "1d04d934-dcdb-526f-b570-2dbcc6fce5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d04d934-dcdb-526f-b570-2dbcc6fce5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437705} :answer-field/id #uuid "b474b307-7652-5024-93ce-3d6d184d1386" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65dc72a9-ac06-5f1a-bf28-b0607daf1e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437706} {:answer/id #uuid "a136b1c8-384b-51aa-80e4-6450fc364d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65dc72a9-ac06-5f1a-bf28-b0607daf1e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437706} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c7e673-aa45-56c5-83bc-18fb8c69889f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0467504c-f5ba-5c65-b935-abce7630a7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437708} {:answer/id #uuid "fab9a31b-ee2c-52cf-8167-c9659890b4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0467504c-f5ba-5c65-b935-abce7630a7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437708} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654b8c1-2a6a-54eb-b934-803e7b9c7856" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438883}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f81692c-bc38-55e0-9228-b9c2fd15506d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123866" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=2x\\,i-3(x+y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i+{e}^{y-x}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x$ and $Q=-3(x+y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q={e}^{y-x}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{y-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402ff24c-ce68-5100-bc6f-ce08b50c1b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437378} {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0832f4-ea5d-510d-80b2-f2b601f5cea1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} {:answer/id #uuid "aadf4a2f-a9fb-5a4e-a7c3-c50425746fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} :answer-field/id #uuid "245451c8-c2d8-5660-b868-914d6a09643c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3af17a-9e86-50af-944e-c33c42e3d28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437382} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd21fc02-2d66-5119-b27a-c74692938a6c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438720}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104574" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(8x-3y)\\,i+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519cca11-1af0-5c38-ac09-9cdf3e9eb8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439999} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3485904-6c14-5182-a07e-0e8536e160b9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb20b90e-716b-5744-aa1c-1bc38cc09c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440001} {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002} :answer-field/id #uuid "908db421-59c3-5473-9555-1804770c666a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e2896f-89e3-56ed-85e3-9569e0e553b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440003} {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bd3f31-bc54-5cfa-8930-506cacf26495" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440359}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179601 :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51d0cea4b75dffe5eef6ccf46e5156716eb1381d20b4193d811acfe83278094.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the curve and constant vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =                 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                                 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $\\mathbf F$ works with the particle for $x >        0,$ while $\\mathbf F$  works against the particle for $x <           0.$ \n\n![](images/67/673798bb131da05a07b3f47894340cbbc143fa6a248823bcfbdf6b5a5238efb4.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                 0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/24/24884d31b2b9aa8d94b11bccfaab1fa31e62a34f4e5183676c79390923f1f243.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =      0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853 :topic/math-academy-id 2108 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions Over Parametric Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b67b2e53-a0c8-5a8b-ab4b-ba652386711f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177772} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7605c9-de7f-5827-a732-cd950df6aaf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177773} {:answer/id #uuid "461655a4-20ba-5050-8819-a2d4ac27734d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177774} {:answer/id #uuid "547ccee9-b44b-5235-8cd6-c0d5e5ab7f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177775} {:answer/id #uuid "78ac1024-eff4-5445-b74f-f7d6bacbd27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177776}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8b91b8c0-4c4a-59fa-a6e4-99f749af022a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177771}] :question/id #uuid "9b9b6def-9db3-53b4-b1f4-7747acfa7a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-125364" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28492e5f-19b4-5757-9d39-0a839e1cd010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459497} {:answer/id #uuid "28528a48-293d-52d1-843c-d4ff315aef34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28492e5f-19b4-5757-9d39-0a839e1cd010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459497} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5c61b9-3843-5dd3-92b0-873626260a5d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f54574f-6fd8-5eb5-9310-72c1143f6efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459499} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d6bd3f-6edc-52da-9997-bbd134508244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1d6bd3f-6edc-52da-9997-bbd134508244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459500} :answer-field/id #uuid "668c3e19-0580-5d17-90d1-02011568ce2a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460616} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6aa09ec-ff05-50f8-950f-485b72db9ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459501} {:answer/id #uuid "79e22fbd-6db9-5639-8366-60b6e8a7bc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79e22fbd-6db9-5639-8366-60b6e8a7bc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459502} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b845de-7f7f-521c-bf75-76f02fd4110e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460617}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "678d2be6-52f5-5dc7-bd50-f78db318a713" :question/math-academy-id "q-125410" :question/problem "![](images/82/82e3ae63881637f258ea92238bbcfc70f2e0993c17d74ce950d6457ffcd95039.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/d3/d363cf00c4901fa0c29bb9521cdd2ea3c12c377dd8e38306df0d5a2837915a36.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5799e631e7eb45ec5a9711ef1569d51b376d8c764a3226caa2676cdb968a6f5.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a312728-759b-5a18-bc9a-7a468e2c468e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462181} {:answer/id #uuid "b006ccd8-dbf9-52a5-8de6-8656396b4437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a312728-759b-5a18-bc9a-7a468e2c468e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462181} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09c4b26-8176-500e-9d72-7fa8141a088c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822889f3-1878-5472-943f-bc21421aa019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462183} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cd909-af16-5666-9ee5-08e066d042c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cd909-af16-5666-9ee5-08e066d042c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462184} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45bb2d9-7504-598f-8971-17193667fabf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462845} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c27fd31-ee21-5aab-a03c-e98f38a45ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462185} {:answer/id #uuid "e661237d-bcf6-5e01-b2dc-8992ab58d984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e661237d-bcf6-5e01-b2dc-8992ab58d984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462186} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebc3f15f-2730-55a5-bdbe-6cc6317a24d4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a58b62d-04d0-5a47-b1b2-408fc180c352" :question/math-academy-id "q-125487" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da7dc42ad4e9eb5b6df7c42f06d7a0f7767820693f8e99e073c7b8bd7e93441.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/14/14e94a2c5d0faabff1beeadeccb6999aa2edd71fce06fff8a2655db74e585eba.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c54487081f2150216b7e41b1351f0f7b19c794b140297789f19d9afe830bd7b1.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d53d1d6-b5c4-5b67-bf25-7d27d099f803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462187} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea8a4ee-7a15-5906-a035-85b03d8a5bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d53d1d6-b5c4-5b67-bf25-7d27d099f803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462187} :answer-field/id #uuid "c81ca5e2-2c88-5480-af0e-02088df15e01" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4889ea-7df9-5050-8467-49e0a39fea58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462189} {:answer/id #uuid "77388560-2b05-5177-b365-d77d025c0040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77388560-2b05-5177-b365-d77d025c0040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462190} :answer-field/id #uuid "e54e19c2-5a75-508b-8a0e-b667cad3e56c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462848} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21087b76-e92c-5bb7-b96b-7c74da65d9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462191} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4cb97b-af71-57cc-ae8c-5ba9597eb620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c4cb97b-af71-57cc-ae8c-5ba9597eb620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462192} :answer-field/id #uuid "432876d0-b578-5b13-9052-5619b6ddf8f1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35e675be-a5b1-5d4c-9024-2339a508b912" :question/math-academy-id "q-125491" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0daaf51b1aab267c11b8457b0e7e1480ff5aabd3acaa15a07f54625c6d8103d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/17/1764bfc51a25475162a70bad7239a36f5d099aa6157c71ed7ed3e02e474eb906.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/05/059731f0cac61f19382172192c6a9f7f366ecb758767797f9bc8311faa3865af.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "534628ec-e086-5d85-8e31-fc04c99a5f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462193} {:answer/id #uuid "aa45667f-b444-5719-9295-4e64b477febd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa45667f-b444-5719-9295-4e64b477febd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462194} :answer-field/id #uuid "046a9634-5223-5c56-bcba-e814a194fb0c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462850} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac9a279-64d9-5123-a34a-eb6c0ad0dfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462195} {:answer/id #uuid "47acd328-c108-50ad-87f5-983bb154f370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac9a279-64d9-5123-a34a-eb6c0ad0dfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462195} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5a4d84f-2772-565a-a292-2aecf13d4c1b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c04105d-6659-5935-8cfc-bd3c43a1dd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462197} {:answer/id #uuid "7122b5b4-4c71-5060-bbaa-0aae72dd956c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c04105d-6659-5935-8cfc-bd3c43a1dd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462197} :answer-field/id #uuid "3056dddf-f8f4-5529-949a-844cf3556faf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e666f143-8faf-54d3-a90e-9cb72a05e2aa" :questi